<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Cloudship in gz. One tilting rod carries both thrusters, so thrust is
  collective and a single servo vectors it; a reversible tail thruster does
  yaw. That is the airframe the flight side already flies
  (init.d/airframes/2507_cloudship: CA_AIRFRAME 16, CA_AIRSHIP_GRP 0,
  CA_AIRSHIP_TAIL 1), and it had no gz simulator: the posix gazebo-classic
  Cloudship (2519) is still on the old CA_AIRFRAME 9 rotor geometry. So this
  is the first plant the collective-and-tail path of the airship allocator
  can be flown against.

  Every length and mass is the gazebo-classic model's own measurement
  (models/cloudship/cloudship.sdf.jinja): a 4.7 x 1.6 m hull of 5.97 m^3 with
  its center of volume 2.22 m from the nose, a 0.4 x 0.17 x 0.1 gondola 2 m
  from the nose and 0.8486 m under that center, a 1.05 kg payload, four
  0.465 x 0.39 m fins from 3.635 m and 0.5634 m off the axis, thrusters
  +-0.45 m either side of the gondola, 13" props good for 1.62 kg each and a
  5" tail prop for 0.24 kg. The inertia is NOT that model's: its parallel-axis
  terms take the wrong offsets (its gondola ixx is built from the x offset and
  its izz from the z one), so the tensor here is recomputed from the same
  bodies about the real center of mass, ixz and all.

  Frame and buoyancy follow airship_base: x forward, y port, z up, the origin
  on the ground under the gondola, and lift from the collision volumes at
  1.225 kg/m^3. The drawn hull is the true 4.7 x 1.6 m ellipsoid and displaces
  6.300 m^3; the real hull holds 5.97, so the collision ellipsoid is the drawn
  one scaled by 0.981925, which with the gondola's 0.0068 m^3 puts the lift at
  7.31480 kg against a mass of 7.3148. The ventral fin reaches 5.5 cm below
  the gondola, which is the vehicle's shape and not a mistake; the gondola
  still carries the ground contact, so the fin passes through the ground when
  the hull is resting on it.

  This is cloudship_rigid with fluid added mass and nothing else, and it is a
  full copy of that file rather than a merge include of it, because
  <fluid_added_mass> is a property of base_link's <inertial>, which a plain
  merge include cannot change. The copy differs from cloudship_rigid by that one
  block, its model name and this paragraph; an edit to either file belongs in
  both. The fluid
  physics come as variants named by what they add, in a fixed order:
    cloudship_am            fluid added mass (a full copy: <fluid_added_mass>
                            lives in the link's <inertial>, which a plain
                            merge include cannot change)
    cloudship_fin           fin lift, four lift-drag plugins
    cloudship_drag          hull drag and rotational damping
    cloudship_am_fin        added mass and fins together: added mass brings
                            the Munk moment, and the fins are what oppose it
    cloudship_am_fin_drag   everything, the plant to tune against
  Every variant but _am is a thin merge include of the model with one token
  fewer (cloudship_fin and cloudship_drag wrap cloudship_rigid, cloudship_am_fin
  wraps cloudship_am, cloudship_am_fin_drag wraps cloudship_am_fin), so the
  vehicle is written in cloudship_rigid and once more in this copy, kept equal to
  that file; the drag block is written once,
  in cloudship_drag, and the fin block once more than that, in the two _fin
  variants, held identical the same way.
  The thruster assembly's 0.30 kg comes out of the gondola's 3.3, which is
  where the classic model leaves it; the split and the moving links' inertias
  are placeholders. Prop links are pitched 90 deg so link +Z, the motor
  plugin's thrust axis, points forward; the tail prop is rolled -90 deg so its
  +Z points to port, which swings the nose to starboard, PX4's positive yaw.
-->
<sdf version="1.10">
  <model name="cloudship_am">
    <self_collide>false</self_collide>
    <static>false</static>
    <link name="base_link">
      <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <!-- Hull shell, lifting gas, gondola, payload and four fins, each
             about its own center and summed about theirs. -->
        <pose>0.0155 0 0.4183 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>7.0148</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>2.3196</ixx>
          <iyy>6.8533</iyy>
          <izz>5.6703</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0.3598</ixz>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
        <!-- Fluid added mass of the hull, Lamb's prolate-spheroid values for
             THIS hull's fineness ratio a/b = 2.9375: k1 0.12555 axial, k2 0.79930
             transverse, k' 0.45438 rotational, on the displaced mass rho V =
             7.3065 kg and the displaced fluid's transverse inertia
             rho V (a^2 + b^2) / 5 = 8.6826 kg m^2 of the collision ellipsoid
             itself. The gazebo-classic template carries k1 0.04662, k2 0.9147,
             k' 0.75567 instead, which are Lamb's values for a/b = 5.875 =
             4.7 / 0.8, the hull's length over its radius where Lamb's ratio
             is semi-length over radius; they are not used, for the same
             reason the inertia above is recomputed rather than copied.
             The tensor is diagonal about the hull's centre of volume and this
             element is written about the link origin, h = 0.8986 m below the
             hull axis, so it picks up the same offset terms the drag block
             does: xq = h m_x, yp = -h m_y, pp = h^2 m_y, qq = I_q + h^2 m_x
             (T = 1/2 v_P^T A v_P with v_P = v_O + w x d, d = (0, 0, h)).
             The engine folds this into the spatial inertia, Coriolis terms
             and all, so it brings the Munk moment with it: -(m_y - m_x) u v
             = -4.9228 u v N m of yaw in this y-port, z-up frame, the nose
             turning away from the velocity. That is what the _fin variants'
             fins are for, and cloudship_am_fin carries both. -->
        <fluid_added_mass>
          <xx>0.91729</xx>
          <yy>5.84009</yy>
          <zz>5.84009</zz>
          <xq>0.82428</xq>
          <yp>-5.24790</yp>
          <pp>4.71576</pp>
          <qq>4.68595</qq>
          <rr>3.94525</rr>
        </fluid_added_mass>
      </inertial>
      <!-- Buoyancy volume 5.96447 m^3: the drawn ellipsoid scaled to the
           hull's real 5.97 m^3 less the gondola's own displacement. dartsim
           logs "Ellipsoid is not a supported collision geomerty" and builds a
           16x16 mesh instead; that is its own notice and not a problem here,
           because the lift comes from the SDF shape's analytic volume and the
           ground contact comes from the gondola. -->
      <collision name="hull_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.8986 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <ellipsoid>
            <radii>2.30752 0.78554 0.78554</radii>
          </ellipsoid>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <!-- Ground contact; buoyancy volume 0.0068 m^3 -->
      <collision name="gondola_collision">
        <pose>0.22 0 0.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.4 0.17 0.1</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <contact>
            <ode>
              <min_depth>0.001</min_depth>
              <max_vel>0</max_vel>
            </ode>
          </contact>
          <friction>
            <ode />
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="hull_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0.8986 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <ellipsoid>
            <radii>2.35 0.8 0.8</radii>
          </ellipsoid>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.9 0.9 0.92 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.9 0.9 0.92 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="gondola_visual">
        <pose>0.22 0 0.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.4 0.17 0.1</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.25 0.25 0.3 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.25 0.25 0.3 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="payload_visual">
        <pose degrees="true">-0.18 0 0.1146 90 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.05</radius>
            <length>0.12</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.15 0.15 0.15 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.15 0.15 0.15 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <!-- Cruciform tail, visual only here: no collision and no lift-drag,
           so it adds no weathercock stiffness, exactly as airship_base's
           does. The _fin variants give these four boxes lift. -->
      <visual name="fin_port_visual">
        <pose>-1.6475 0.7584 0.8986 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.465 0.39 0.01</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_starboard_visual">
        <pose>-1.6475 -0.7584 0.8986 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.465 0.39 0.01</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_top_visual">
        <pose>-1.6475 0 1.6570 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.465 0.01 0.39</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_bottom_visual">
        <pose>-1.6475 0 0.1402 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.465 0.01 0.39</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <sensor name="air_pressure_sensor" type="air_pressure">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>50</update_rate>
        <air_pressure>
          <!-- Noise modeled after BMP390 -->
          <pressure>
            <noise type="gaussian">
              <mean>0</mean>
              <stddev>3</stddev>
            </noise>
          </pressure>
        </air_pressure>
      </sensor>
      <sensor name="magnetometer_sensor" type="magnetometer">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>100</update_rate>
        <magnetometer>
          <!-- 3mgauss RMS = 3e-7 tesla, noise modeled after IIS2MDC -->
          <!-- Noise is in the units of the field: tesla with use_units_gauss=false (lta.sdf's Magnetometer
               system, as PX4's gz_bridge server.config), gauss with the gz-sim default -->
          <x>
            <noise type="gaussian">
              <stddev>3e-7</stddev>
            </noise>
          </x>
          <y>
            <noise type="gaussian">
              <stddev>3e-7</stddev>
            </noise>
          </y>
          <z>
            <noise type="gaussian">
              <stddev>3e-7</stddev>
            </noise>
          </z>
        </magnetometer>
      </sensor>
      <sensor name="imu_sensor" type="imu">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>250</update_rate>
        <imu>
          <angular_velocity>
            <!-- Noise modeled after IIM42653, 0.05 deg/s in rad/s -->
            <x>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.0008726646</stddev>
              </noise>
            </x>
            <y>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.0008726646</stddev>
              </noise>
            </y>
            <z>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.0008726646</stddev>
              </noise>
            </z>
          </angular_velocity>
          <linear_acceleration>
            <!-- Noise modeled after IIM42653, 0.65/0.70 mg-rms in m/s^2 -->
            <x>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.00637</stddev>
              </noise>
            </x>
            <y>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.00637</stddev>
              </noise>
            </y>
            <z>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.00686</stddev>
              </noise>
            </z>
          </linear_acceleration>
        </imu>
      </sensor>
      <sensor name="navsat_sensor" type="navsat">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>30</update_rate>
      </sensor>
    </link>
    <!-- One rod, both thrusters: thrust is collective and one servo vectors
         it. The stop is +-pi for the same reason as the big airship's: a pod
         is a servo, and the bridge caps the command at +-180 anyway. -->
    <link name="thruster_rod">
      <pose>0.32 0 0.05 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.2</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0135</ixx>
          <iyy>0.0005</iyy>
          <izz>0.0135</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="thruster_rod_visual">
        <pose degrees="true">0 0 0 90 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.01</radius>
            <length>0.9</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="tilt_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>thruster_rod</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 -1 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-3.141593</lower>
          <upper>3.141593</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>0.01</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>
    <link name="prop_starboard">
      <pose degrees="true">0.32 -0.45 0.05 0 90 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.04</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0003</ixx>
          <iyy>0.0003</iyy>
          <izz>0.0005</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="prop_starboard_visual">
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.02 0.3302 0.02</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="prop_starboard_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>thruster_rod</parent>
      <child>prop_starboard</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-1e+16</lower>
          <upper>1e+16</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <spring_reference>0</spring_reference>
          <spring_stiffness>0</spring_stiffness>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>
    <link name="prop_port">
      <pose degrees="true">0.32 0.45 0.05 0 90 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.04</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0003</ixx>
          <iyy>0.0003</iyy>
          <izz>0.0005</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="prop_port_visual">
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.02 0.3302 0.02</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="prop_port_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>thruster_rod</parent>
      <child>prop_port</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-1e+16</lower>
          <upper>1e+16</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <spring_reference>0</spring_reference>
          <spring_stiffness>0</spring_stiffness>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>
    <!-- At the trailing edge of the ventral fin root, which is where the
         classic model puts it: r_fin_x - fin_root_chord/2 back and r_fin_rad
         down, so -1.6475 - 0.2325 and 0.8986 - 0.5634. It was on the hull
         axis at the fin station, which is 0.2325 m too far forward, 0.5634 m
         too high, and buried inside the hull - the ellipsoid still has a
         0.550 m local radius there. The height is what mattered: on the axis
         the tail's thrust acts 0.49 m above the centre of mass and banks the
         hull 1.9 degrees every time it yaws, where at the fin root it acts
         0.07 m below it and the bank is 0.27 degrees the other way. A yaw
         thruster goes in the lower fin precisely so that yaw does not roll
         the vehicle. -->
    <link name="prop_tail">
      <pose degrees="true">-1.88 0 0.3352 -90 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.02</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.00002</ixx>
          <iyy>0.00002</iyy>
          <izz>0.00004</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="prop_tail_visual">
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.015 0.127 0.015</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="prop_tail_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>prop_tail</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-1e+16</lower>
          <upper>1e+16</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <spring_reference>0</spring_reference>
          <spring_stiffness>0</spring_stiffness>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-position-controller-system"
      name="gz::sim::systems::JointPositionController">
      <joint_name>tilt_joint</joint_name>
      <sub_topic>servo_0</sub_topic>
      <p_gain>2.0</p_gain>
      <i_gain>0.0</i_gain>
      <d_gain>0.1</d_gain>
    </plugin>
    <!-- 7.0632e-06 * 1500^2 = 15.89 N per thruster, 31.8 N on a 7.3 kg
         vehicle, which is the classic model's 1.62 kg each. The two turn
         opposite ways so their reaction couples cancel at equal thrust, and
         the collective group never splits them. timeConstant is the classic
         model's mot_tau; momentConstant, rotorDragCoefficient and
         rollingMomentCoefficient are still the x500 quadrotor's. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-multicopter-motor-model-system"
      name="gz::sim::systems::MulticopterMotorModel">
      <jointName>prop_starboard_joint</jointName>
      <linkName>prop_starboard</linkName>
      <turningDirection>ccw</turningDirection>
      <timeConstantUp>0.05</timeConstantUp>
      <timeConstantDown>0.05</timeConstantDown>
      <maxRotVelocity>1500.0</maxRotVelocity>
      <motorConstant>7.0632e-06</motorConstant>
      <momentConstant>0.016</momentConstant>
      <commandSubTopic>command/motor_speed</commandSubTopic>
      <motorNumber>0</motorNumber>
      <rotorDragCoefficient>8.06428e-05</rotorDragCoefficient>
      <rollingMomentCoefficient>1e-06</rollingMomentCoefficient>
      <rotorVelocitySlowdownSim>10</rotorVelocitySlowdownSim>
      <motorType>velocity</motorType>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-multicopter-motor-model-system"
      name="gz::sim::systems::MulticopterMotorModel">
      <jointName>prop_port_joint</jointName>
      <linkName>prop_port</linkName>
      <turningDirection>cw</turningDirection>
      <timeConstantUp>0.05</timeConstantUp>
      <timeConstantDown>0.05</timeConstantDown>
      <maxRotVelocity>1500.0</maxRotVelocity>
      <motorConstant>7.0632e-06</motorConstant>
      <momentConstant>0.016</momentConstant>
      <commandSubTopic>command/motor_speed</commandSubTopic>
      <motorNumber>1</motorNumber>
      <rotorDragCoefficient>8.06428e-05</rotorDragCoefficient>
      <rollingMomentCoefficient>1e-06</rollingMomentCoefficient>
      <rotorVelocitySlowdownSim>10</rotorVelocitySlowdownSim>
      <motorType>velocity</motorType>
    </plugin>
    <!-- 2.3544 N at full command, the classic model's 0.24 kg prop, 1.902 m
         behind the center of mass for 4.48 N m of yaw. Its thrust axis is
         +Y, so a positive command swings the nose to starboard, and it sits
         0.07 m below the centre of mass, so a full-command yaw also banks the
         hull 0.27 degrees.

         This is the vehicle's only yaw authority, so it has to go both ways,
         and it is the one motor here that is not a MulticopterMotorModel.
         That plugin reverses on a negative rotor velocity, but no negative
         ever reaches it: the bridge's SIM_GZ_EC_MIN/MAX live in a uint16_t
         inside MixingOutput, which truncates a negative and then swaps the
         pair because min now exceeds max. So the sign has to live here.
         duty_cycle + bidirectionalMotor centres the non-negative 1100..1900
         the bridge does send, giving a signed -1..1 reference, and the two
         thrust polynomials are 2.3544 * ref^2 either side of it. Same
         mechanism and the same duty range as uuv_bluerov2_heavy.

         The polynomials must carry four coefficients even though only the
         quadratic one is non-zero, and the constant term must be zero: the
         plugin takes ref >= minCommand, so a non-zero constant would be a
         standing thrust at neutral.

         Only the elements below are read in this mode. maxRotVelocity,
         motorConstant, momentConstant, rotorDragCoefficient,
         rollingMomentCoefficient and the two time constants belong to the
         plugin's kVelocity branch and are not carried here, however much
         uuv_bluerov2_heavy carries them: the thrust comes from the
         polynomial and the joint velocity is written straight from the
         reference. That last one is a real loss worth saying out loud - this
         tail has no lag, where the classic template gives its prop tau
         0.025. -->
    <plugin filename="libGenericMotorModelPlugin.so"
      name="gz::sim::systems::GenericMotorModel">
      <jointName>prop_tail_joint</jointName>
      <linkName>prop_tail</linkName>
      <turningDirection>ccw</turningDirection>
      <commandSubTopic>command/motor_speed</commandSubTopic>
      <actuator_number>2</actuator_number>
      <rotorVelocitySlowdownSim>0.025</rotorVelocitySlowdownSim>
      <motorType>force_polynomial</motorType>
      <controlMethod>duty_cycle</controlMethod>
      <minCommand>0.0</minCommand>
      <minDutyCycle>1100</minDutyCycle>
      <maxDutyCycle>1900</maxDutyCycle>
      <bidirectionalMotor>1</bidirectionalMotor>
      <positiveThrustPolynomial>[0.0, 0.0, 2.3544, 0.0]</positiveThrustPolynomial>
      <negativeThrustPolynomial>[0.0, 0.0, 2.3544, 0.0]</negativeThrustPolynomial>
    </plugin>
  </model>
</sdf>
